Expect the listed sizes for the encoding the fixtures were written in

The locale fixtures in conftest.py write "Dies ist eine Datei über Katzen."
and its Swedish sibling with Path.write_text() and no encoding, so their sizes
follow Python's default text encoding. test_list.py picks its expected sizes
by operating system instead, assuming Windows writes one byte per character.
That holds only while Python writes a single-byte code page such as cp1252,
where upstream's CI runs. Under PYTHONUTF8=1, which a machine whose code page
lacks ä and ü (cp1251 here) needs before the fixtures can be written at all,
the three --detailed listings fail. Key the choice on the encoding; under
cp1252 nothing changes.

Upstream: not submitted yet
Applies-to: all environments

--- a/test/test_list.py
+++ b/test/test_list.py
@@ -1,7 +1,14 @@
+import codecs
+import locale
